","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The query results returned by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" contains all of the information about the query that you'll need for templating and any other usage of the data:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const result = useQuery('todos', fetchTodoList)","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result"},{"type":"text","value":" object contains a few very important states you'll need to be aware of to be productive. A query can only be in one of the following states at any given moment:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isLoading"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status === 'loading'"},{"type":"text","value":" - The query has no data and is currently fetching"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isError"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status === 'error'"},{"type":"text","value":" - The query encountered an error"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isSuccess"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status === 'success'"},{"type":"text","value":" - The query was successful and data is available"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isIdle"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status === 'idle'"},{"type":"text","value":" - The query is currently disabled (you'll learn more about this in a bit)"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Beyond those primary states, more information is available depending on the state of the query:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" - If the query is in an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isError"},{"type":"text","value":" state, the error is available via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" property."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" - If the query is in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"success"},{"type":"text","value":" state, the data is available via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" property."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isFetching"},{"type":"text","value":" - In any state, if the query is fetching at any time (including background refetching) "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isFetching"},{"type":"text","value":" will be "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"most"}]},{"type":"text","value":" queries, it's usually sufficient to check for the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isLoading"},{"type":"text","value":" state, then the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isError"},{"type":"text","value":" state, then finally, assume that the data is available and render the successful state:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function Todos() {\n  const { isLoading, isError, data, error } = useQuery('todos', fetchTodoList)\n\n  if (isLoading) {\n    return <span>Loading...</span>\n  }\n\n  if (isError) {\n    return <span>Error: {error.message}</span>\n  }\n\n  // We can assume by this point that `isSuccess === true`\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {data.map(todo => (\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If booleans aren't your thing, you can always use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" state as well:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function Todos() {\n  const { status, data, error } = useQuery('todos', fetchTodoList)\n\n  if (status === 'loading') {\n    return <span>Loading...</span>\n  }\n\n  if (status === 'error') {\n    return <span>Error: {error.message}</span>\n  }\n\n  // also status === 'success', but \"else\" logic works, too\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {data.map(todo => (\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"further-reading","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Further Reading"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For an alternative way of performing status checks, have a look at the "},{"type":"link","href":"../community/tkdodos-blog#4-status-checks-in-react-query","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Community Resources"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"query-basics","text":"Query Basics","level":2},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further Reading","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Query Basics\n\nA query is a declarative dependency on an asynchronous source of data that is tied to a **unique key**. A query can be used with any Promise based method (including GET and POST methods) to fetch data from a server. If your method modifies data on the server, we recommend using [Mutations](https://react-query.tanstack.com/docs/guides/mutations) instead.\n\nTo subscribe to a query in your components or custom hooks, call the `useQuery` hook with at least:\n\n- A **unique key for the query**\n- A function that returns a promise that:\n  - Resolves the data, or\n  - Throws an error\n\n```js\nimport { useQuery } from 'react-query'\n\nfunction App() {\n  const info = useQuery('todos', fetchTodoList)\n}\n```\n\nThe **unique key** you provide is used internally for refetching, caching, and sharing your queries throughout your application.\n\nThe query results returned by `useQuery` contains all of the information about the query that you'll need for templating and any other usage of the data:\n\n```js\nconst result = useQuery('todos', fetchTodoList)\n```\n\nThe `result` object contains a few very important states you'll need to be aware of to be productive. A query can only be in one of the following states at any given moment:\n\n- `isLoading` or `status === 'loading'` - The query has no data and is currently fetching\n- `isError` or `status === 'error'` - The query encountered an error\n- `isSuccess` or `status === 'success'` - The query was successful and data is available\n- `isIdle` or `status === 'idle'` - The query is currently disabled (you'll learn more about this in a bit)\n\nBeyond those primary states, more information is available depending on the state of the query:\n\n- `error` - If the query is in an `isError` state, the error is available via the `error` property.\n- `data` - If the query is in a `success` state, the data is available via the `data` property.\n- `isFetching` - In any state, if the query is fetching at any time (including background refetching) `isFetching` will be `true`.\n\nFor **most** queries, it's usually sufficient to check for the `isLoading` state, then the `isError` state, then finally, assume that the data is available and render the successful state:\n\n```js\nfunction Todos() {\n  const { isLoading, isError, data, error } = useQuery('todos', fetchTodoList)\n\n  if (isLoading) {\n    return <span>Loading...</span>\n  }\n\n  if (isError) {\n    return <span>Error: {error.message}</span>\n  }\n\n  // We can assume by this point that `isSuccess === true`\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {data.map(todo => (\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nIf booleans aren't your thing, you can always use the `status` state as well:\n\n```js\nfunction Todos() {\n  const { status, data, error } = useQuery('todos', fetchTodoList)\n\n  if (status === 'loading') {\n    return <span>Loading...</span>\n  }\n\n  if (status === 'error') {\n    return <span>Error: {error.message}</span>\n  }\n\n  // also status === 'success', but \"else\" logic works, too\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {data.map(todo => (\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}\n```\n## Further Reading\n\nFor an alternative way of performing status checks, have a look at the [Community Resources](../community/tkdodos-blog#4-status-checks-in-react-query).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: queries\ntitle: Queries\n---\n\n## Query Basics\n\nA query is a declarative dependency on an asynchronous source of data that is tied to a **unique key**. A query can be used with any Promise based method (including GET and POST methods) to fetch data from a server. If your method modifies data on the server, we recommend using [Mutations](https://react-query.tanstack.com/docs/guides/mutations) instead.\n\nTo subscribe to a query in your components or custom hooks, call the `useQuery` hook with at least:\n\n- A **unique key for the query**\n- A function that returns a promise that:\n  - Resolves the data, or\n  - Throws an error\n\n```js\nimport { useQuery } from 'react-query'\n\nfunction App() {\n  const info = useQuery('todos', fetchTodoList)\n}\n```\n\nThe **unique key** you provide is used internally for refetching, caching, and sharing your queries throughout your application.\n\nThe query results returned by `useQuery` contains all of the information about the query that you'll need for templating and any other usage of the data:\n\n```js\nconst result = useQuery('todos', fetchTodoList)\n```\n\nThe `result` object contains a few very important states you'll need to be aware of to be productive. A query can only be in one of the following states at any given moment:\n\n- `isLoading` or `status === 'loading'` - The query has no data and is currently fetching\n- `isError` or `status === 'error'` - The query encountered an error\n- `isSuccess` or `status === 'success'` - The query was successful and data is available\n- `isIdle` or `status === 'idle'` - The query is currently disabled (you'll learn more about this in a bit)\n\nBeyond those primary states, more information is available depending on the state of the query:\n\n- `error` - If the query is in an `isError` state, the error is available via the `error` property.\n- `data` - If the query is in a `success` state, the data is available via the `data` property.\n- `isFetching` - In any state, if the query is fetching at any time (including background refetching) `isFetching` will be `true`.\n\nFor **most** queries, it's usually sufficient to check for the `isLoading` state, then the `isError` state, then finally, assume that the data is available and render the successful state:\n\n```js\nfunction Todos() {\n  const { isLoading, isError, data, error } = useQuery('todos', fetchTodoList)\n\n  if (isLoading) {\n    return <span>Loading...</span>\n  }\n\n  if (isError) {\n    return <span>Error: {error.message}</span>\n  }\n\n  // We can assume by this point that `isSuccess === true`\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {data.map(todo => (\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nIf booleans aren't your thing, you can always use the `status` state as well:\n\n```js\nfunction Todos() {\n  const { status, data, error } = useQuery('todos', fetchTodoList)\n\n  if (status === 'loading') {\n    return <span>Loading...</span>\n  }\n\n  if (status === 'error') {\n    return <span>Error: {error.message}</span>\n  }\n\n  // also status === 'success', but \"else\" logic works, too\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {data.map(todo => (\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}\n```\n## Further Reading\n\nFor an alternative way of performing status checks, have a look at the [Community Resources](../community/tkdodos-blog#4-status-checks-in-react-query).\n"}
